====Roland Burris of Illinois====
{{Main|United States Senate elections in Illinois, 2010}}
Mr. Burris is not seeking re-election in 2010.

The primary elections on February 2, 2010 produced the following winners:
* Democrat: State Treasurer [[Alexi Giannoulias]]
* Republican: U.S. Representative [[Mark Steven Kirk|Mark Kirk]] of [[Illinois' 10th congressional district]]
* Green: [[LeAlan Jones]], journalist

Currently on the Ballot in Illinois is:
* Democrat: [[Alexi Giannoulias]]
* Republican: U.S. Representative [[Mark Steven Kirk|Mark Kirk]] of [[Illinois' 10th congressional district]]
* Green: [[LeAlan Jones]]
* Libertarian: [[Michael Labno]]

The race between Kirk and Giannoulias has become increasingly hostile.  Kirk has criticized the business practices of Giannoulias and the family bank he worked for, Broadway Bank, which was seized by Federal regulators for missing a deadline to raise capital after accusations of corrupt lending practices.  Giannoulias has pounced on revelations that Kirk exaggerated his military record.

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 61.85 percent of the vote in Illinois while [[John McCain]] received 36.74 percent, a 25.11-percent margin of victory for the former Democratic incumbent of this seat.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000001674 |title=Profile for Sen. Roland W. Burris, Illinois Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1937-08-03 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

'''Other Parties'''

The Libertarian Party has selected Michael Labno as its candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thegreenpapers.com/G09/IL.phtml |title=Illinois 2009 General Election |publisher=Thegreenpapers.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> To qualify for the ballot, Labno's campaign had to gather  by June 21, 2010. After turning in In August 2010, the Libertarian Party of Illinois defended petitions against a series of challenges during an eight day line by line review in Springfield, Illinois. At the end of the review, Th Libertarian Party of Illinois had over 33,000 valid signatures, 8,000 more than the minimum of 25,000 valid signatures required for new parties and independent candidates according Illinois ballot access laws.

The Constitution Party has nominated [[Randy Stufflebeam|Randall C. Stufflebeam]] as its candidate. Stufflebeam did not meet the minimum requirement for petition signatures after review and has elected to continue as a write-in candidate.

The following candidates filed nominating petitions: Carl Officer of the Practical Party and [[Andy Martin (U.S. politician)|Andy Martin]] of the Reform Party. Those filing as independents include Christopher Pedersen, Will Boyd, Shon-Tiyon "Santiago" Horton, and Corey Dabney.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.pantagraph.com/news/state-and-regional/illinois/article_b4ee4116-7e46-11df-b926-001cc4c002e0.html |title=The Pantagraph |publisher=The Pantagraph |date= June 22, 2010|accessdate=2010-06-25}}</ref> The period for objecting to independent and new party candidates is still open.

Independent Conservative Robert L. "Bob" Zadek has successfully filed the Declaration of Intent to be a Write-In Candidate in all 102 Illinois Counties and the eight requisite cities for the General Election and is in the process of filing the same paperwork for the upcoming Special Election for the Vacancy on November 2, 2010 as well.

----

====Evan Bayh of Indiana====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Indiana, 2010}}
Two-term incumbent Democrat [[Evan Bayh]] and former two-term [[Governor of Indiana]], surprisingly announced that he would not seek reelection in 2010.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://twitter.com/huffingtonpost/statuses/9144595143 |title=Twitter / Huffington Post: ALERT: Sen. Evan Bayh (D-I) |publisher=Twitter.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

U.S. Representative [[Brad Ellsworth]] of [[IN-08|Indiana's 8th congressional district]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref name="nyt-20100220">{{cite news | url = http://www.nytimes.com/2010/02/20/us/politics/20brfs-CONGRESSMANE_BRF.html | title = Indiana: Congressman Enters Race for Bayh's Senate Seat | date = 2010-02-20 | author = Bloomberg news | work = [[The New York Times]] }}</ref>

Lobbyist and former U.S. Senator and [[Dan Coats]] is the Republican nominee.<ref>[http://www.indianasnewscenter.com/news/local/84004092.html "Dan Coats Confirms Bid For U.S. Senate"], February 10, 2010.</ref>

Running under the Libertarian Party is [[Rebecca Sink-Burris]], a former teacher from [[Bloomington, Indiana]].

Indiana also has two independents attempting to make the ballot for the general election: Don Harris<ref>http://www.publicparty.info/</ref> and Timothy Frye<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thegreenpapers.com/G10/IN |title=Indiana 2010 Midterm Election |publisher=Thegreenpapers.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] narrowly carried Indiana with 49.85 percent of the vote while [[John McCain]] received 48.82 percent, a 1.03-percent margin of victory for the Democrat. Former U.S. Representative [[Bob Barr]] (R-[[Georgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]]), who ran as the [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] nominee, received 1.06 percent of the vote in Indiana.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/individual/#INP00 | work=CNN | title=Local and National Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>

----

====Byron Dorgan of North Dakota====
{{Main|United States Senate election in North Dakota, 2010}}
Incumbent Democrat [[Byron Dorgan]] announced January 5, 2010 that he would not seek reelection in 2010.<ref>[http://blogs.usatoday.com/onpolitics/2010/01/north-dakota-sen-byron-dorgan-to-retire.html "North Dakota Sen. Byron Dorgan To Retire"], USA Today.</ref><ref name=bismarcktribune>{{cite news|url=http://www.bismarcktribune.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/article_73d96b4e-fa7d-11de-8f0d-001cc4c002e0.html|title=N.D.'s Sen. Dorgan won't run for re-election in 2010|last=Bjorke|first=Christopher|coauthors=Jenny Michael|date=5 January 2010|work=The Bismarck Tribune|accessdate=29 January 2010}}</ref> In 2004, he won with 68 percent of the vote.

[[North Dakota Senate|State Senator]] [[Tracy Potter]] is the only Democrat to have formally announced a candidacy for Dorgan's vacant U.S. Senate seat.<ref>[https://secure.forumcomm.com/?publisher_ID=1&article_id=267879 INFORUM | Fargo, ND<!-- Bot generated title -->]</ref> All other major candidates have announced that they will not be running, including former [[North Dakota Attorney General]] [[Heidi Heitkamp]],{{Citation needed|date=June 2010}} and 2006 [[North Dakota Secretary of State]] nominee [[Kristen Hedger]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2010}} Potter won the Democratic primary election with no competition.

On the Republican side, popular [[Governor of North Dakota|Governor]] [[John Hoeven]] has announced his candidacy.<ref>{{cite web|last=Trygstad |first=Kyle |url=http://www.realclearpolitics.com/politics_nation/2010/01/nd_sen_poll_hoeven_begins_race.html |title=Politics Nation - ND Sen Poll: Hoeven Begins Race With Lead |publisher=RealClearPolitics |date=2010-01-15 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Hoeven won the Republican nomination at the state primary convention on March 22.

The Libertarian Party of North Dakota will be represented on the ballot by Keith Hanson.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thegreenpapers.com/G10/ND |title=North Dakota 2010 Midterm Election |publisher=Thegreenpapers.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 53.15 percent of the vote in North Dakota while [[Barack Obama]] received 44.50 percent, an 8.65-percent margin of victory for the Republicans. Independent [[Ralph Nader]] received 1.32 percent of the vote in North Dakota.

