====Carte Goodwin of West Virginia====

{{Main|United States Senate special election in West Virginia, 2010}}

Senator [[Robert Byrd]] died on June 28, 2010. Democratic governor [[Joe Manchin]] appointed [[Carte Goodwin]] to serve until the special election. Goodwin has announced he will not run in the special election.

Both parties held their primaries on August 28. On the Democratic side, popular Governor Joe Manchin is running.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://voices.washingtonpost.com/thefix/senate/west-virginia-gov-joe-manchin.html | work=The Washington Post | title=W.Va. Gov. Joe Manchin launches Senate campaign; Capito on deck}}</ref> Manchin was challenged in the primary by 95-year old former representative and former [[Secretary of State of West Virginia|Secretary of State]] [[Ken Hechler]] <ref>{{cite web|last=Rudin |first=Ken |url=http://www.npr.org/blogs/politicaljunkie/2010/07/21/128674420/capito-won-t-run-in-west-virginia-but-hechler-95-will |title=Capito Won't Run For The Senate In West Virginia, But Hechler, 95, Will : Political Junkie |publisher=NPR |date=2010-07-21 |accessdate=2010-08-21}}</ref> and 2008 Senate candidate Sheirl Fletcher.<ref>{{cite web|author=webmaster@wvmh.com |url=http://wowktv.com/story.cfm?func=viewstory&storyid=83216 |title=Sheirl Fletcher Announces Run for Senate Seat - WOWK-TV |publisher=WOWKTV.com |date= |accessdate=2010-08-21}}</ref> Joe Manchin won the primary nomination with over seventy percent of the Democratic vote.

On the Republican side, 2006 Republican nominee against Byrd [[John Raese]] is running.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/0710/40107.html |title=Raese is in for W.Va. Senate special - Shira Toeplitz |publisher=Politico.Com |date= |accessdate=2010-08-21}}</ref> State Senator Clark Barnes was another potential candidate. Representative Shelley Moore Capito announced that she will not seek the seat.<ref>[http://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5gJNsYvo9r6oANYKQgXuqtlBabdMQD9H3G54G0 ]{{Dead link|date=August 2010}}</ref> Raese won the Republican nomination with over seventy percent of that vote.

The [[Mountain Party]] of West Virginia have announced the candidacy of [[Jesse Johnson (politician)|Jesse Johnson]] in the special election.  Johnson is currently running unopposed for the Mountain Party's nomination.

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] carried West Virginia with 55.60 percent of the vote while [[Barack Obama]] received 42.51 percent, a 13.09-percent margin of victory for the Republican.

