====Sam Brownback of Kansas====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Kansas, 2010}}
Two-term incumbent Republican [[Sam Brownback]], who sought a bid for the [[Republican Party (United States) presidential primaries, 2008|2008 Republican presidential nomination]], has stated that he will not run for reelection in 2010 because of self-imposed term limits.<ref name="Brownback 2010">{{cite web | title=Brownback announces Senate retirement | work=[[Joplin Globe]] | url=http://www.joplinglobe.com/statenews/local_story_353235338.html |date=2008-12-18}}</ref>

On the Republican side, U.S. Representative [[Jerry Moran]] of [[KS-01|Kansas's 1st congressional district]] has filed papers with the [[Federal Elections Commission]] (FEC) to run for Brownback's vacant U.S. Senate seat.<ref name="Moran">{{cite web|title=Is that the distant sound of primary war drums? Moran, Tiahrt talk U.S. Senate 2010 | work= [[Kansas City Star]] | url= http://primebuzz.kcstar.com/?q=node/15584 | date=November 9, 2008 | accessdate = November 9, 2008 |author=Steve Kraske}}{{dead link|date=October 2010}}</ref> Another U.S. Representative, [[Todd Tiahrt]] of [[KS-04|Kansas's 4th congressional district]], has also announced his candidacy for the seat, setting up what is expected to be a bruising GOP primary.<ref name="Tiahrt">{{cite web|title=Kansan Tiahrt makes Senate bid official | publisher = The Hill | url = http://thehill.com/leading-the-news/kansan-tiahrt-makes-senate-bid-official-2009-01-31.html | date = January 31, 2009 | accessdate = January 31, 2009 | author= Jeffrey Young}}</ref>  The August 2 Republican primary was surprisingly close, but Moran managed a narrow victory over Tiahrt and will face Baker University Assistant Dean [[Lisa Johnston]] in the general election.

U.S. Congressman [[Jerry Moran]] won the Republican primary and will face Democrat [[Lisa Johnston]] and two minor party candidates in the general election

The Reform Party has nominated Joe Bellis.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.bellisforsenate.com/ |title=Joe Bellis - 2010 Candidate United States Senate for Kansas |publisher=Bellisforsenate.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 56.48 percent of the vote in Kansas while [[Barack Obama]] received 41.55 percent, a 14.93-percent margin of victory for the Republican.

