====Jim Bunning of Kentucky====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Kentucky, 2010}}
Two-term incumbent Republican [[Jim Bunning]] was narrowly reelected (51-49 percent) in 2004 against his Democratic opponent, then-[[Kentucky Senate|State Senator]] [[Daniel Mongiardo]], after several of Bunning's controversial actions made the race close.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docid=news-000003094607 |title=Will Bunning Swing and Miss in 2010? |publisher=CQ Politics |date=2009-04-08 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Bunning had intended to run for reelection, but the Republican Party had long been lukewarm to his relatively weak candidacy.<ref name="Bunning 2010">{{cite web|url=http://www.courier-journal.com/article/20090124/NEWS01/901240456|title=Bunning's war cupboard nearly bare|date=2009-01-24|work=[[Lexington Herald]]}}{{dead link|date=October 2010}}</ref> On July 27, 2009, Bunning announced that he would retire rather than seek a third term.<ref name="courier-journal.com">[http://www.courier-journal.com/article/20090727/NEWS0106/907270346/Sen.+Bunning+says+he+will+retire++won+t+seek+third+term Bunning will not seek 3rd term]{{Dead link|date=June 2010}}</ref>

The primary elections on May 18, 2010 produced the following winners:
*Republican: [[Rand Paul]], the son of libertarian-leaning U.S. Representative and 2008 Republican presidential candidate [[Ron Paul]] (R-Texas), defeated Kentucky Secretary of State [[Trey Grayson]]. Senate Minority Leader [[Mitch McConnell]] (R-Kentucky) had endorsed Grayson, but Paul had the enthusiasm and momentum of the [[Tea Party movement]] behind him, including an endorsement from former [[Governor of Alaska]] and 2008 Republican vice presidential nominee [[Sarah Palin]].<ref name="Conway">[http://www.courier-journal.com/article/20090409/NEWS01/90409011 ]{{Dead link|date=June 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.politico.com/blogs/scorecard/0409/Elite_bashing_in_Kentucky.html?showall |title=The Scorecard: Elite bashing in Kentucky |publisher=Politico.Com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>
*Democratic: Attorney General [[Jack Conway (politician)|Jack Conway]] defeated Lieutenant Governor [[Daniel Mongiardo]].

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 57.37 percent of the vote in Kentucky while [[Barack Obama]] received 41.15 percent, a 16.22-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=KT | work=CNN | title=State Election Results&nbsp;— Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>

