====Kit Bond of Missouri====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Missouri, 2010}}
Four-term incumbent Republican [[Kit Bond]] was reelected with 56 percent of the vote in 2004. He announced that he would not seek reelection.<ref name="Bond 2010">{{cite news | first=Manu | last=Raju | title=Bond will not seek another term | date=2009-01-08 | url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/0109/17213.html | work=[[The Politico|Politico]] | accessdate=2009-01-08}}</ref>

The Republican field consists of U.S. Representative [[Roy Blunt]], the former House Majority Whip before the [[Tom DeLay campaign finance investigation|Tom Delay scandal]] who represents [[MO-07|Missouri's 7th congressional district]] based in Southwest Missouri, has announced his candidacy.<ref name="Blunt">{{cite web|url=http://www.politico.com/blogs/scorecard/0209/Blunt_running_for_Senate.html?showall |title=The Scorecard: 2008 congressional campaign news and analysis |publisher=Politico.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Former State Treasurer [[Sarah Steelman]], who sought but narrowly lost the Republican gubernatorial nomination in August 2008 to [[Kenny Hulshof]], also considered a run for the Republican nomination. However, Steelman announced on September 16, 2009 that she would not be a candidate for any public office in 2010.<ref>[http://www.stltoday.com/blogzone/political-fix/dc-download/2009/01/sarah-steelman-eyes-bonds-senate-seat-in-2010 Sarah Steelman Eyes Bonds' Senate Seat In 2010]{{dead link|date=October 2010}}, stltoday.com</ref> Former U.S. Senator [[Jim Talent]] (R), who was narrowly unseated in 2006 by former State Auditor [[Claire McCaskill]] (D), announced that he, too, would not run.<ref>[http://news.yahoo.com/s/ap/20090211/ap_on_re_us/missouri_senate Missouri's Talent won't seek return to Senate]{{Dead link|date=June 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Keller |first=Rudi |url=http://www.semissourian.com/article/20090109/NEWS01/701109998 |title=semissourian.com |publisher=semissourian.com |date=2009-01-10 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Blunt is joined by 10 other Republicans seeking the GOP nomination, including [[Missouri Senate|State Senator]] [[Chuck Purgason]]. Blunt won by a heavy margin in the primary on August 3, 2010.

The Progressive Party of Missouri has selected [[Midge Potts]] as its candidate. She announced her candidacy in June 2009.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.progressivepartymo.org/gpmo/index.php?module=pagemaster&PAGE_user_op=view_page&PAGE_id=4&MMN_position=8:8 |title=Election News - Progressive Party of Missouri |publisher=Progressivepartymo.org |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

On the Democratic side, [[Missouri Secretary of State|Secretary of State]] [[Robin Carnahan]], the daughter of former U.S. Senator [[Jean Carnahan]] and former [[Governor of Missouri|Governor]] [[Mel Carnahan]], entered the race on February 3, 2009,<ref name="Carnahan">{{cite web|author=Robin Carnahan is IN for U.S. Senate in 2010 |url=http://primebuzz.kcstar.com/?q=node/16941 |title=kcstar.com |publisher=Primebuzz.kcstar.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}{{dead link|date=October 2010}}</ref> and is widely considered the frontrunner for the Democratic nomination. In 2008, Carnahan won her second term as Secretary of State with nearly 1.7 million votes, the most votes ever cast for a single candidate in Missouri state history, winning all but 10 of Missouri's 116 counties. Two other Democrats will join Carnahan on the primary ballot. Carnahan won by a heavy margin in the primary on August 3, 2010.

The Constitution Party of Missouri will have three candidates from whom to choose at its convention: Jerry Beck,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://electbeckussenate.com/ |title=Elect Jerry Beck |publisher=Electbeckussenate.com |date=2010-03-15 |accessdate=2010-06-13}} {{Dead link|date=September 2010|bot=H3llBot}}</ref> Joe Martellaro,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.martellaroforsenate.com/ |title=Joseph Martellaro for US Senate - Less Taxes, Less Government, More Liberty |publisher=Martellaroforsenate.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> and Mike Simmons.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.simmonsforussenate.com/ |title=Mike Simmons for US Senate 2010 - Welcome |publisher=Simmonsforussenate.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

The Libertarian Party has two choices for its convention: Jonathan Dine<ref>{{cite web|url=http://votedine.com/ |title=Jonathan Dine for US Missouri Senator 2010 |publisher=Votedine.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> and Cisse Spragins.

Glenn Miller<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.whty.org/ |title=Vote for Glenn Miller |publisher=Whty.org |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> is seeking election as a write-in candidate.

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] narrowly carried Missouri with 49.36 percent of the vote while [[Barack Obama]] received 49.23 percent, a 0.13-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000277 |title=Profile for Sen. Christopher S. Bond, Missouri Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1939-03-06 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

