====Judd Gregg of New Hampshire====
{{Main|United States Senate election in New Hampshire, 2010}}
Three-term incumbent Republican [[Judd Gregg]] was reelected in 2004 with 66 percent of the vote.<ref name="cqpolitics.com">{{cite web | url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000314 | title=Profile for Sen. Judd Gregg, New Hampshire Republican | work= CQ Politics}}</ref> Gregg had originally announced his intention to run for a fourth term, but was nominated to the position of [[United States Secretary of Commerce]] by [[President of the United States|President]] [[Barack Obama]] in early February 2009. However, Gregg withdrew his nomination on February 12, 2009, citing "irresolvable conflicts" over policy related to the Commerce Department.<ref name=withdrawal>{{cite news | url=http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2009/02/12/breaking-gregg-withdraws-from-consideration-for-commerce-post | title=Breaking: Gregg Withdraws From Consideration For Commerce Post | work=CNN | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref> Since then, Gregg stated that he would not seek reelection to the U.S. Senate.<ref name="Gregg 2010">{{cite news|url=http://thehill.com/campaign-2008/gregg-not-convinced-to-run-again-2009-04-01.html|title=Gregg says he definitely won't run again|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|author=J. Taylor Rushing |date=April 1, 2009}}</ref>

On the Democratic side, the only announced candidate thus far is U.S. Representative [[Paul Hodes]] who represents [[NH-02|New Hampshire's 2nd congressional district]].<ref name="Hodes">{{cite web | url=http://thehill.com/leading-the-news/rep.-hodes-will-stay-in-new-hampshire-senate-race-2009-02-12.html | title=Rep. Hodes will stay in New Hampshire Senate race | work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]}}</ref>

On the Republican side:
*Attorney General [[Kelly Ayotte]] — On July 7, 2009, she announced that she was seriously considering running,<ref>{{ cite news | url=http://www.unionleader.com/article.aspx?headline=Kelly+Ayotte++to+explore+2010+Senate+run&articleId=0b0b4a3c-d0e0-46e2-9207-30e4d88ba997 | work= Manchester Union Leader | title=Kelly Ayotte To Explore 2010 Senate Run}}</ref> and on July 20, she filed paperwork to form an exploratory committee.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.fox44.net/Global/story.asp?S=10756891 |title=fox44.net |publisher=fox44.net |date=2009-07-20 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}{{dead link|date=October 2010}}</ref>
*Jim Bender
*Bill Binnie, businessman
*Ovide Lamontagne, attorney and 1996 GOP gubernatorial nominee<ref name=lamontagne>{{cite web | url = http://www.politico.com/blogs/scorecard/1109/Lamontagne_announces_Senate_campaign.html | title = Lamontagne announces Senate campaign | publisher = Politico | date = 2009-11-08 | accessdate = 2009-11-25 | author = Josh Kraushaar}}</ref>

The Libertarian Party of New Hampshire has nominated self-employed realtor and perennial candidate Kenneth Blevens.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://kblevens.org/ |title=Welcome &#124; Ken Blevens for U.S. Senate 2010 |publisher=Kblevens.org |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

