====Arlen Specter of Pennsylvania====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Pennsylvania, 2010}}
Five-term incumbent Democrat [[Arlen Specter]] had been reelected as a Republican by a 53% to 42% margin in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000429 |title=Profile for Sen. Arlen Specter, Pennsylvania Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> On April 28, 2009, Specter announced that he would leave the Republican Party and seek a sixth term in 2010 as a Democrat.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.politico.com/blogs/bensmith/0409/Specter_switching_parties.html?showall |title=Ben Smith's Blog: Specter switching parties |publisher=Politico.Com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

Based on polling, the 2004 primary results and a smaller, more conservative Pennsylvania Republican primary electorate, Specter judged that he would lose the Republican primary, and since Pennsylvania law would bar him from running as an independent under such circumstances, he decided to switch parties. As a long-standing moderate, Specter had had tensions with the conservative base of the GOP dating back to 1987 when he played a key role in derailing President [[Ronald Reagan]]'s nomination of [[Robert Bork]] to the U.S. Supreme Court. Recently, Specter had further antagonized the GOP base in Pennsylvania by voting in favor of the [[American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009]] otherwise known as the Economic Stimulus Bill. In return for joining the Democratic Party, President [[Barack Obama]], Vice President [[Joe Biden]], [[Governor of Pennsylvania|Governor]] [[Ed Rendell]] and Senate Majority Leader [[Harry Reid]] all discouraged candidates from challenging Specter in the Democratic primary.

Retired U.S. Navy Rear Admiral and current U.S. Representative [[Joe Sestak]] of [[PA-07|Pennsylvania's 7th congressional district]] announced on August 4, 2009, that he would challenge Specter for the Democratic nomination despite the support for Specter amongst the Democratic establishment.<ref name="Sestak">{{cite web|url=http://www.politico.com/blogs/scorecard/0509/Report_Sestak_intends_to_challenge_Specter.html |title=Report: Sestak intends to challenge Specter |publisher=Politico.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Specter was thought to be favored until polls two weeks before the Democratic primary began showing a [[Tie (draw)|dead heat]] between him and Sestak.<ref>See [[United States Senate election in Pennsylvania, 2010#Polling]].</ref> On May 18, 2010, Sestak defeated Specter in the Democratic primary by a 54% to 46% margin.<ref name="Sestak Wins">{{cite web | url=http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/37210973/ns/politics-decision_2010 | title=Sestak beats Specter in Pa. Dem Senate race | author=MSNBC.com Staff | date=2010-05-18 | publisher=MSNBC}}</ref>

On the Republican side, former U.S. Representative and former President of the [[Club for Growth]] [[Pat Toomey]], who came within 1.7 percent of defeating Specter in the 2004 GOP primary, announced that he would run for the Republican nomination. Former [[Governor of Massachusetts|Governor]] and 2008 Republican presidential candidate [[Mitt Romney]] has endorsed Toomey.<ref name="Toomey">{{cite web | url=http://blogs.cqpolitics.com/eyeon2010/2009/04/specters-primary-fight-its-off.html#more | title=Specter's Primary Fight: It's Official | author= CQ Staff | date=2009-04-15 | publisher=CQ Politics}}</ref> Other Republican candidates include businessman Larry Murphy,<ref name="Murphy">{{cite web|url=http://www.sharonherald.com/local/local_story_059223214.html |title=A challenge from the right: Murphy sets sights on Specter's Senate seat |publisher=Sharonherald.com |date=2009-02-28 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> who challenged Specter in the 1998 GOP Senate primary, 1994 and 1998 [[Constitution Party (United States)|Constitution Party]] gubernatorial candidate [[Peg Luksik]],<ref name="Luksik">[http://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5ggevr_xkeWwIJjJivXQ4eQp-OwzgD96R8TU80 Conservative Peg Luksik to challenge Arlen Specter]{{Dead link|date=March 2010}}</ref> and retired [[Federal Air Marshal Service|Federal Air Marshal]] Robert Townsend.<ref name="Townsend">{{cite web|url=http://votecommonsense.us |title=Rob Townsend for U.S. Senate |publisher=Votecommonsense.us |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 54.47 percent of the vote in Pennsylvania while [[John McCain]] received 44.15 percent, a 10.32-percent margin of victory for the Democrat.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=PA | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>

===<span style="color:#f22;">Republican incumbents defeated in primary election (2 seats)</span>===

----

====Lisa Murkowski of Alaska====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Alaska, 2010}}
[[File:Senate Ratings Table 2010 AK.svg ‎]]

Incumbent Republican [[Lisa Murkowski]], who was appointed in 2002 by [[Governor of Alaska|Governor]] [[Frank Murkowski]] (her father), was elected to a full term in 2004 with 48.6% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000017262 |title=Profile for Sen. Lisa Murkowski, Alaska Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1957-05-22 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

Murkowski announced that she would seek reelection with fundraising aid from former [[Governor of Alaska|Governor]] [[Sarah Palin]].<ref name=murkowski>{{cite news | url = http://www.miamiherald.com/news/politics/AP/story/990010.html | work = [[The Miami Herald]] | title = Palin to aid Murkowski's 2010 Senate campaign | date = April 8, 2009}}{{dead link|date=October 2010}}</ref> However, Palin endorsed and supported [[Joe Miller (Alaska politician)|Joe Miller]] in the Republican primary election campaign.  After the primary votes were counted on election day, August 24, Joe Miller was barely ahead. However, the absentee ballots were not counted until a week later, on August 31. With the absentee votes counted, Miller defeated Murkowski in the primary. Murkowski conceded the election to Miller following the counting of the absentee ballots.<ref>{{cite news|first=Shira |last=Toeplitz |url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/0810/41652.html |title=Lisa Murkowski concedes defeat to Joe Miller |publisher=Politico.Com |date=August 30, 2010 |accessdate=2010-10-31}}</ref>

On the Democratic side, Mayor [[Scott McAdams]] ([[Sitka, Alaska|Sitka]]) was nominated in the primary.

On September 17, 2010, Murkowski announced that she would run a write-in campaign against Miller and McAdams.<ref name="google1">{{cite news | first = Becky | last = Bohrer | title = Murkowski mounting write-in bid for Alaska Senate | date = 2010-09-18 | url = http://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5iXP5SqYD7mXezWparpit8qa6EsrwD9IAC5801 | agency = Associated Press | accessdate = 2010-09-18 | quote = Murkowski faces tough odds with her [[Write-in candidate|write-in]] candidacy. She has lost support from members within the Republican establishment, who are backing the Republican nominee, Joe Miller.}}{{dead link|date=October 2010}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 59.42% of the vote in Alaska while [[Barack Obama]] received 37.89%, a 21.53-percent margin for the Republican.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=AK | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref> In the [[United States Senate election in Alaska, 2008|2008 senate election]],  Democrat [[Mark Begich]] with 47.77% of the vote beat incumbent Republican [[Ted Stevens]] with 46.52% of the vote due to an ethics scandal.

