====Bob Bennett of Utah====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Utah, 2010}}
Three-term incumbent Republican [[Robert Foster Bennett|Bob Bennett]] was reelected with 69 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000494 |title=Profile for Sen. Robert F. Bennett, Utah Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Bennett intended to run for reelection.<ref name="Bennett 2010">{{cite web | url=http://deseretnews.com/article/1,5143,705268417,00.html | title=Bennett is gearing up for another Senate bid | author=Bob Bernick Jr. | date=2008-12-06 | accessdate=2008-12-08 | publisher=DesertNews.com}}</ref> In May 2009, Bennett released an early advertisement, in which [[Mitt Romney]] expressed his support for Bennett, to discourage a primary challenge.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://thehill.com/campaign-2008/sen.-bennett-launches-ads-featuring-romney-2009-05-18.html |title=Campaign |publisher=TheHill.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

At the May 8 Republican nominating convention, Bennett was defeated, finishing third in the balloting and resulting in him not being eligible to run in the Republican primary that was held June 22, 2010 and featured the top two finshers, [[Tim Bridgewater]] and [[Michael S. Lee|Mike Lee]]. Bennett's defeat in the convention somewhat raised Democrats' hopes of winning the seat in this heavily Republican state.<ref>[http://www.sltrib.com/news/ci_14751894 ''Salt Lake Tribune'' report]{{dead link|date=October 2010}} and [http://www.politico.com/news/stories/0310/34968.html Politio.com analysis.]</ref>  In a mild surprise, Lee narrowly won the Republican primary and will face Utah Alcoholic Beverage Control Commission Chairman Sam Granato in the general election.<ref>Weigel, David. [http://voices.washingtonpost.com/right-now/2010/06/mike_lee_wins_in_utah.html Mike Lee wins in Utah, and so does the tea party], ''[[Washington Post]]'', June 23, 2010.</ref><ref>[http://electionresults.utah.gov/xmlData/300010.html Utah Election Results: Republican for US Senate], ''[[Utah|Utah Secretary of State]]'', June 22, 2010.</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 62.24 percent of the vote in Utah while [[Barack Obama]] received 34.22 percent, a 28.02-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=UT | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>
