====Blanche Lincoln of Arkansas====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Arkansas, 2010}}
[[File:Senate Ratings Table 2010 AR.svg ‎]]

Two term incumbent Democrat [[Blanche Lincoln]] was reelected with 56 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref name="ReferenceB">{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000018 |title=Profile for Sen. Blanche Lincoln, Arkansas Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1960-09-30 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Lincoln will seek reelection and has already held a major fundraiser with [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]] [[Joe Biden]].<ref name=lincoln>{{cite web | url =  http://www.katv.com/news/stories/0309/603992.html | title = Vice President in Little Rock to Support Senator Lincoln | date = 2009-03-15 | publisher = KATV}}{{dead link|date=October 2010}}</ref>

Lincoln received a tough primary challenge from Lieutenant Governor [[Bill Halter]] who entered the race on March 1, 2010<ref>{{cite news| url=http://content.usatoday.com/communities/onpolitics/post/2010/03/lt-gov-halter-to-challenge-sen-lincoln-in-ark-democratic-primary/1 | work=USA Today | title=Lt. Gov. Halter to challenge Sen. Lincoln in Ark. Democratic primary | date=March 1, 2010 | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref> and raised more than $1 million in his first 48 hours.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2010/03/quick-cash-for-halter-liberals-rally-to-raise-1-million/36964/ |title=Quick Cash for Halter: Liberals Rally to Raise $1 Million - Politics |publisher=The Atlantic |date=2010-03-03 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> In the primary, Lincoln finished with less than a majority and faced a runoff with Halter. On June 8, Lincoln won the runoff by a 52% to 48% margin.<ref name="cnn060810">{{cite news |url=http://www.cnn.com/2010/POLITICS/06/08/tuesday.primaries/index.html?hpt=T1 |title=Battle lines drawn in Tuesday's primaries |accessdate=2010-06-09 |author=CNN|date=2010-06-08}}</ref>

In the Republican primary, U.S. Representative [[John Boozman]] of [[AR-03|Arkansas's 3rd congressional district]] based in Northwest Arkansas finished with an absolute majority to become the Republican Senate nominee.<ref name=boozman>{{cite web | url = http://news.bostonherald.com/news/us_politics/view/20100130source_boozman_to_challenge_blanche_lincolns_arkansas_senate_seat/srvc=home&position=recent | title = Source: Boozman to challenge Blanche Lincoln's Arkansas Senate seat | work = Boston Herald | date = 2010-01-30 | accessdate = 2010-02-03}}</ref>

Other candidates who sought the Republican nomination included:
*[[Arkansas Senate|State Senator]] [[Kim Hendren]]
*[[Arkansas Senate|State Senator]] [[Gilbert Baker (Arkansas politician)|Gilbert Baker]]
*Retired [[United States Army|U.S. Army]] Colonel Conrad Reynolds
*Curtis Coleman, a businessman from [[Little Rock, Arkansas|Little Rock]]
*Fred Ramey, a real estate investment firm owner
*Tom Cotton, an alum from [[Harvard Law School]]
*University of Arkansas Housing Director and Washington County Tea Party Vice Chair Randy Alexander
*Tom Cox, founder of the Arkansas branch of the [[Tea Party protests|Tea Party movement]]<ref name=republicanarkansas>{{cite web | url = http://www.southernpoliticalreport.com/storylink_98_998.aspx | title = Arkansas: Obama proposals make trouble for Lincoln | publisher = Southern Political Report | date = 2009-09-08}}</ref>
*Former State Senator [[Jim Holt (Arkansas politician)|Jim Holt]]<ref name=holt>[http://www.arktimes.com/blogs/arkansasblog/2010/01/jim_holt_to_make_senate_race.aspx Arkansas Blog: Jim Holt to make Senate race]{{Dead link|date=June 2010}}</ref>

Former Green Beret Trevor Drown has announced his candidacy as an independent.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.couriernews.com/archived_story.php?ID=22133&Search=Trevor%20Drown |title=The Courier, Russellville, Ark |publisher=Couriernews.com |date=2009-07-27 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

Mayor John Gray of [[Greenland, Arkansas|Greenland]] is seeking the Green Party nomination.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thegreenpapers.com/G09/AR.phtml |title=Arkansas 2009 General Election |publisher=Thegreenpapers.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 58.72 percent of the vote in Arkansas while [[Barack Obama]] received 38.86 percent, a 19.86-percent margin of victory for the Republican.

