====Barbara Boxer of California====
{{Main|United States Senate election in California, 2010}}
[[File:Senate Ratings Table 2010 CA.svg ‎]]

Three-term incumbent Democrat [[Barbara Boxer]] (D) was reelected with 58 percent of the vote in 2004,<ref name="ReferenceC">{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000081 |title=Profile for Sen. Barbara Boxer, California Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1940-11-11 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> but had difficult races in 1992 and 1998. In February 2007, she announced that she would seek a fourth term in 2010.<ref name="Boxer 2010">{{cite news|url=http://www.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/blogs/sfgate/detail?blogid=14&entry_id=13649 |title=Boxer: I'm in to win -- in 2010 |publisher=[[SFGate.com]] |date=2007-02-17 |accessdate=2008-11-06 |author=John Wildermuth}}</ref> Boxer won the Democratic Senate primary on June 8 with 80 percent of the vote. "The surprise runner-up: Hollywood producer Brian Quintana, who snatched almost 15% of the vote."<ref name="KQED Public Media">{{cite web|url=http://blogs.kqed.org/capitalnotes/2010/06/09/last-call-for-primary-2010/ |accessdate=2010-07-13|title=Last Call For Primary 2010}}</ref> Blogger [[Mickey Kaus#Senate Run|Mickey Kaus]] received the remaining 5%.

Boxer is facing a very tough challenge from her major opponent, Republican businesswoman [[Carly Fiorina]], according to several recent polls.<ref>{{cite news |first=Kitty |last=Felde |url=http://www.scpr.org/news/2010/06/03/barbara-boxer-could-face-tough-re-election-fight/ |title=Senator Barbara Boxer could face tough re-election fight |publisher=[[KPCC]] |date=2010-06-03 |accessdate=2010-06-03}}</ref> Fiorina, former [[Hewlett-Packard]] CEO, defeated [[California State Assembly|State Assemblyman]] [[Chuck DeVore]], as well as former Congressman [[Tom Campbell (California politician)|Tom Campbell]], for the GOP nomination.

Donald J. "Don" Grundmann has filed paperwork for the American Independent Party nomination.<ref name=autogenerated1>{{cite web|url=http://www.thegreenpapers.com/G10/CA.phtml |title=California 2010 Midterm Election |publisher=Thegreenpapers.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

Jerry L. Carroll has filed as an independent.<ref name="thegreenpapers.com"/>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 60.94 percent of the vote in California while [[John McCain]] received 36.91 percent.<ref name="ReferenceC"/>

The last time California elected a Republican to the U.S. Senate was in 1988, when [[Pete Wilson]] won a second term. He resigned in 1992 upon being elected governor, and the state has since had both its Senate seats occupied by Democrats.

