====Michael Bennet of Colorado====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Colorado, 2010}}
Following the resignation of one-term U.S. Senator [[Ken Salazar]] (D) to become [[President of the United States|President]] [[Barack Obama]]'s [[United States Secretary of the Interior|Secretary of the Interior]],<ref name="Senate Confirms Seven Obama Nominees, Delays Clinton">{{cite web|url=http://www.bloomberg.com/apps/news?pid=20601087&sid=aFtD02yqOtMw&refer=home |title=bloomberg.com |publisher=bloomberg.com |date=2009-01-20 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> [[Governor of Colorado|Governor]] [[Bill Ritter]] selected [[Michael Bennet]], the former [[Superintendent (education)|Superintendent]] of [[Denver Public Schools]], to fill Salazar's seat for the remainder of his term.<ref>{{cite news | first=Karen | last=Crummy | title=Michael Bennet chosen as next Senator | date=2009-01-02 | url=http://www.denverpost.com/breakingnews/ci_11355396 | work=[[Denver Post]] | accessdate=2009-01-02}}</ref> Bennet won his party's nomination in the August 10, 2010 primary election, fighting off a stiff challenge by former Speaker of the [[Colorado House of Representatives]] [[Andrew Romanoff]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://ac360.blogs.cnn.com/2010/08/11/georgia-runoff-too-close-to-call-bennet-gets-big-win-in-colorado/ |title=Bennet gets big win in Colorado |publisher=CNN |date=2010-08-11 |accessdate=2010-08-11}}</ref> Romanoff received the endorsement of former President [[Bill Clinton]] while Bennet has been endorsed by President Obama.

On the Republican side, former Lieutenant Governor [[Jane Norton]],<ref>[http://www.krdo.com/Global/story.asp?S=11136916 ]{{Dead link|date=June 2010}}</ref> [[Weld County, Colorado|Weld County]] District Attorney [[Ken Buck]],<ref name="Buck">{{cite web|url=http://www.greeleytribune.com/article/20090428/NEWS/904289949/1008/NONE&parentprofile=1001 |title=Buck announces bid for U.S. Senate seat |publisher=Greeleytribune.com |date=2009-04-28 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> former [[Colorado Senate|State Senator]] Tom Wiens,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://rockymountainright.com/?q=node/981 |title=Tom Wiens running for Senate |publisher=Rocky Mountain Right |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|author=Brittanicus |url=http://coloradocommunitynewspapers.com/articles/2009/07/17/highlands_ranch_herald/news/23_cm_wiens_hr.txt |title=Colorado Community Newspapers Online - News Around Town > Archives > Highlands Ranch Herald > News > Wiens ‘leaning’ toward U.S. Senate run |publisher=Coloradocommunitynewspapers.com |date=2009-07-17 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> and businessman Cleve Tidwell<ref name="Tidwell">{{cite web|url=http://facethestate.com/buzz/15972-tidwell-announces-2010-run-senate |title=Tidwell announces 2010 run for Senate |publisher=Facethestate.com |date=2009-05-08 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> have announced their candidacies. In addition, radio talk show host Dan Caplis is considered a potential candidate.<ref>http://www.timescall.com/news_story.asp?ID=15402</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Crummy |first=Karen E. |url=http://www.denverpost.com/politics/ci_11560209 |title=Suthers nixes bid for governor, Senate in 2010 |publisher=Denverpost.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Weins has since then dropped out of the race and has endorsed Buck.

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 53.66 percent of the vote in Colorado while [[John McCain]] received 44.71 percent, an 8.95-percent margin of victory for the Democrat.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000030182 |title=Profile for Sen. Michael Bennet, Colorado Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1964-11-28 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

The state has elected both Republicans and Democrats to the Senate. While the Centennial State currently has Democrats filling both of its Senate seats, it was represented by two Republican senators as recently as 2005.
