====Kirsten Gillibrand of New York====
{{Main|United States Senate special election in New York, 2010}}
Incumbent Democrat [[Kirsten Gillibrand]] was appointed by [[Governor of New York|Governor]] [[David Paterson]] to fill this seat on January 23, 2009, after then-Senator [[Hillary Clinton|Hillary Rodham Clinton]] resigned to take up her appointment as [[United States Secretary of State|U.S. Secretary of State]].<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/2009/POLITICS/01/23/gillibrand.profile/index.html | work=CNN | title=N.Y. governor names Clinton successor - CNN.com | date=January 23, 2009 | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref> Gillibrand will serve until the seat is filled in the November 2010 special election, with her term ending January 2013.<ref>[http://www.centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/article.php?id=LJS2009021901 ]{{Dead link|date=June 2010}}</ref> Gillibrand has filed paperwork to run in the 2010 election.<ref name=fec>{{cite web | url = http://query.nictusa.com/cgi-bin/fecimg/?S0NY00410 |title=Gillibrand, Kirsten Elizabeth}}</ref> Jonathan Tasini, a progressive challenger who ran for U.S. Senate in 2006, is running against Gillibrand in the Democratic primary. Former U.S. Representative [[Harold Ford, Jr.]] of [[Tennessee]] has opted not to run.<ref name="reuters">{{cite news|url=http://www.reuters.com/article/idUSTRE62106F20100302|title=Harold Ford won't seek New York Senate seat|last=Cooney|first=Peter|date=March 1, 2010|publisher=Reuters|accessdate=March 9, 2010}}</ref>  Gillibrand won the primary against attorney Gail Goode by a margin of four to one.

On the Republican side, David Malpass, president of Encima Global and a former chief economist at Bear Stearns, filed paperwork on March 16, 2010 to run.<ref name="cnbc.com">{{cite web|url=http://www.cnbc.com/id/35892640 |title=News Headlines |publisher=Cnbc.com |date=2010-03-16 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Also in March 2010, former U.S. Representative [[Joseph J. DioGuardi|Joe DioGuardi]] indicated that he would enter the 2010 Republican Senate primary.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nydailynews.com/news/politics/2010/03/12/2010-03-12_joe_dioguardi_american_idol_judge_kara_dioguardis_dad_to_run_for_kirsten_gillibr.html |title="Joe DioGuardi, 'American Idol' judge Kara DioGuardi's dad, to run for Kirsten Gillibrand Senate seat," March 12, 2010, New York Dailyl News |publisher=Nydailynews.com |date=2010-03-12 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>  DioGuardi won the primary and will face Gillibrand on November 2.

----

====Chuck Schumer of New York====
{{Main|United States Senate election in New York, 2010}}
Two-term incumbent Democrat [[Chuck Schumer]], the former chairman of the [[Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee|Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee (DSCC)]] was reelected with 71 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000371 |title=Profile for Sen. Charles E. Schumer, New York Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1950-11-23 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> He is unlikely to face strong competition in 2010.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nydailynews.com/news/politics/2009/01/31/2009-01-31_sen_chuck_schumer_unlikely_to_see_compet.html |title=Sen. Chuck Schumer unlikely to see competition in 2010 reelection bid |publisher=Nydailynews.com |date=2009-02-01 |accessdate=2010-06-13 | location=New York | first=David | last=Saltonstall}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 62.88 percent of the vote in New York while [[John McCain]] received 36.03 percent, a 26.85-percent margin of victory for the Democrat.<ref name="cnn.com">{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=NY | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>

----

====Ron Wyden of Oregon====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Oregon, 2010}}
Two-term incumbent Democrat [[Ron Wyden]] (D) was reelected with 64 percent of the vote in 2004 and easily won the Democratic Senate primary nomination in 2010. He will face the winner of the Republican primary, [[Jim Huffman]], a law professor at [[Lewis & Clark Law School]], in the general election.  He will also face [http://workingfamiliesforbrucecronk.org/ Bruce Cronk] of the [http://oregonwfp.org/ Oregon Working Families Party].  <ref name=primresults>{{cite news
|url=http://gov.oregonlive.com/election/2010primary/US-Senate/
|title=Oregon 2010 Primary Results: U. S. Senate
|date=May 18, 2010
|accessdate=May 19, 2010
|work=[[The Oregonian]]
}}</ref>

A May 26 poll by Rasmussen Reports shows Wyden leading Huffman by a 51% to 38% margin.

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 56.75 percent of the vote in Oregon while [[John McCain]] received 40.40 percent, a 16.35-percent margin of victory for the Democrat. Independent [[Ralph Nader]] received 1.02 percent of the vote in Oregon.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=OR | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>

----

====Patrick Leahy of Vermont====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Vermont, 2010}}
Six-term incumbent Democrat [[Patrick Leahy]] was reelected with 71 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000510 |title=Profile for Sen. Patrick J. Leahy, Vermont Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1940-03-31 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Leahy has stated that he will run for reelection in 2010.<ref name=leahy>{{cite web | url = http://www.burlingtonfreepress.com/blog/politics/2009/06/leahy-to-seek-re-election.html | publisher = Burlington Free Press | title = Leahy to seek re-election | date = 2009-06-24 | accessdate = 2009-06-25}}</ref>

Leahy faces a primary challenger in the form of [[Captain (U.S. Navy)|Captain]] Daniel Freilich in the [[United States Naval Reserve]] and a physician specializing in internal medicine.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.danielfreilich.com/freilich_bio.php |title=Dan Freilich's Biography &#124; Physician, Associate Professor, and U.S. Navy Captain |publisher=Danielfreilich.com |date=1963-11-17 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

[[Cannabis in the United States|Pro-marijuana legalization]] activist and perennial candidate Cris Ericson has announced her candidacy representing the [[United States Marijuana Party]].

John A. LaPierre has also announced his candidacy as an [[Independent (politician)|independent]].{{Citation needed|date=October 2009}}

Len Britton has announced his candidacy<ref>{{cite web|url=http://lenbritton.com/ |title=Len Britton for U.S. Senate |publisher=Lenbritton.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> as a Republican.

In [[United States presidential election, 2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 67.46 percent of the vote in Vermont while [[John McCain]] received 30.45 percent, a 37.01-percent margin of victory for the Democrat. Independent [[Ralph Nader]] received 1.03 percent of the vote as well.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=VT | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>

----

====Patty Murray of Washington====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Washington, 2010}}
Three-term incumbent Democrat [[Patty Murray]] was reelected with 55 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000522 |title=Profile for Sen. Patty Murray, Washington Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1950-10-11 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> She has announced that she will be seeking reelection.

[[Dino Rossi]], who narrowly lost the 2004 gubernatorial election and then lost again in 2008 to [[Christine Gregoire]], announced on May 26, 2010 that he would run against Murray.  Other announced Republican candidates include businessman and inventor [http://www.akersforussenate.com/ Paul Akers],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.akersforussenate.com/ |title=Home |publisher=Akers for US Senate |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> energy trader and nuclear engineer [[Craig Williams]]{{dn}}<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.voteWilliams2010.com/ |title=Blog &#124; Craig Williams: Candidate for US Senate |publisher=Votewilliams2010.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>, physician [http://CodayForUSSenate.com/ Art Coday],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://CodayForUSSenate.com |title=Art Coday for US Senate - Washington - Republican |publisher=Codayforussenate.com |date=2010-05-28 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> U.S. Army veteran Wayne Glover,<ref>http://realchange2010.org/</ref> 2000 U.S. House of Representatives candidate Sean Salazar,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sean4senate.com/ |title=Sean Salazar for Senate |publisher=Sean4senate.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> and former pro football player [[Clint Didier]].<ref name=didier>{{cite web | url = http://blog.seattlepi.com/seattlepolitics/archives/189903.asp | title = Didier to run for U.S. Senate against Murray | publisher = Seattle Pi | date = 2010-01-04 | accessdate = 2009-01-23 | author = Joel Connelly}}</ref> Motivational speaker Chris Widener, also a Republican Senate candidate, pulled out from the race on April 23, 2010.<ref name="Widener">{{cite web|url=http://blog.seattlepi.com/seattlepolitics/archives/181621.asp |title=Motivator looks at run against Murray |publisher=Blog.seattlepi.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

Polls show Rossi running strongly against Murray in the general election, though Tea Party-backed candidate, Clint Didier, won the most support in the straw polls at the Washington Republican Party Convention in [[Vancouver, Washington|Vancouver]].

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 57.34 percent of the vote in Washington while [[John McCain]] received 40.26 percent, a 17.12-percent margin of victory for the Democrat.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://uselectionatlas.org/RESULTS/state.php?year=2008&fips=53&f=1&off=0&elect=0 |title=2008 Presidential General Election Results - Washington |publisher=Uselectionatlas.org |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

