====Russ Feingold of Wisconsin====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Wisconsin, 2010}}
Three-term incumbent Democrat [[Russ Feingold]] was reelected with 55 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000533 |title=Profile for Sen. Russ Feingold, Wisconsin Democrat |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1953-03-02 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>  Feingold has announced his campaign staff for reelection and is expected to announce his formal election intentions soon.<ref name=feingold>{{cite web|url=http://www.wispolitics.com/index.iml?Article=149909 | title=Feingold Campaign: Announces core campaign staff | date = 2009-02-20 | accessdate = 2009-02-20 | publisher = Wisconsin Politics | author = Trevor Miller}}</ref>

The Republican nominee elected on September 7th is Ron Johnson, a Fox Valley millionaire businessman and political novice.

Rob Taylor is also running as the Constitution Party nominee.<ref>{{cite web|author=~ James Madison |url=http://www.robtaylorforsenate.com/ |title=Welcome! |publisher=Robtaylorforsenate.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 56.22 percent of the vote in Wisconsin while [[John McCain]] received 42.31 percent, a 13.91-percent margin of victory for the Democrat.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=WI | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>  This election is proving to be Feingold's most difficult.  Recent polling places the Wisconsin race as "Leans GOP"<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rasmussenreports.com/public_content/politics/elections/election_2010/election_2010_senate_elections/wisconsin/election_2010_wisconsin_senate |title=Election 2010: Wisconsin Senate - Rasmussen Reports™ |publisher=Rasmussenreports.com |date= |accessdate=2010-10-31}}</ref>
