====Richard Shelby of Alabama====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Alabama, 2010}}
[[File:Senate Ratings Table 2010 AL.svg ‎]]

Four-term incumbent Republican [[Richard Shelby]], a former Democrat who switched parties in [[Republican Revolution|1994 when Republicans took control of both the U.S. House of Representatives and U.S. Senate]], was reelected with 68% of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000011 |title=Profile for Sen. Richard C. Shelby, Alabama Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1934-05-06 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Shelby's 2010 campaign committee had over $13 million on hand as of September 30, 2008, and will stand for reelection in 2010.<ref name="Shelby 2010">{{cite web | url= http://blog.al.com/live/2009/01/shelby_running_again_spokeswom.html | title=Shelby running again, spokeswoman confirms | date = 01-12-09}}</ref>

The Democratic nominee is William Barnes, a lawyer and Vietnam veteran.

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 60.32% of the vote in Alabama while [[Barack Obama]] received 38.74%, a 21.58-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=AL | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>

----

====John McCain of Arizona====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Arizona, 2010}}
2008 Republican presidential nominee and four-term incumbent Republican [[John McCain]] was reelected with 77% of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000026 |title=Profile for Sen. John McCain, Arizona Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1936-08-29 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> He is running for re-election.

In the Republican primary on August 24, 2010, McCain defeated two opponents:
*Former U.S. Representative [[J. D. Hayworth|J.D. Hayworth]], who announced on February 15, 2010 that he was running.<ref name="thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com">{{cite news| url=http://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/02/15/mccain-challenger-makes-it-official/?scp=2&sq=hayworth&st=cse | work=The New York Times | title=McCain Challenger Makes It Official | first=Bernie | last=Becker | date=February 15, 2010 | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>
*Jim Deakin, U.S. Navy veteran and businessman.<ref name="Deakin">{{cite web|url=http://jimdeakin.com/ |title=Jim Deakin for United States Senate |publisher=Jimdeakin.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

On the Democratic side, Rodney Glassman, former Vice-Mayor of [[Tucson, Arizona|Tucson]] took the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite news| http://results.enr.clarityelections.com/AZ/19539/32216/en/summary.html#val=AZ | work=Secretary of State | title=Arizona - Primary Election Results | accessdate=Aug 26, 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| http://phoenix.org/columns/exclusive-phoenix-org-interview-of-senatorial-candidate-rodney-glassman/#val=AZ | work=Phoenix.org | title=Arizona US Senatorial Race: Rodney Glassman| accessdate=Aug 26, 2010}}</ref> Glassman is a businessman, author, and social entrepreneur. He also serves in the [[Judge Advocate General's Corps]] (JAG) of the [[United States Air Force]]. He defeated three opponents:
*[[Randy Parraz]], a labor attorney from Phoenix.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.parrazforchange.com/ |title=Randy Parraz for U.S Senate :: New Challenges Demand New Leaders |publisher=Parrazforchange.com |date=2008-10-21 |accessdate=2010-08-21}}</ref>
*Cathy Eden, former state legislator and an aide for Governors [[Rose Mofford]] and [[Janet Napolitano]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://edenforaz.com/ |title=HOME - Eden For Arizona |publisher=Edenforaz.com |date= |accessdate=2010-08-21}}</ref>
*[[John Dougherty (Journalist)|John Dougherty]], news columnist from [[Phoenix, Arizona|Phoenix]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.johndougherty2010.com/ |title=JohnDougherty2010.com }}</ref>


In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], McCain won his home state of Arizona with 53.39% of the vote while [[Barack Obama]] won 44.91%, an 8.48-percent margin for the Republican.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=AZ | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>
----

====Johnny Isakson of Georgia====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Georgia, 2010}}
One-term incumbent Republican [[Johnny Isakson]] was elected with 58% of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000132 |title=Profile for Sen. Johnny Isakson, Georgia Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1944-12-28 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Isakson has announced that he will run for reelection.<ref name="briefingroom.thehill.com">{{cite web|url=http://briefingroom.thehill.com/2009/02/18/broun-denies-interest-in-challenging-isakson-sort-of/ |title=Blog Briefing Room - The Hill's Blog Briefing Room |publisher=Briefingroom.thehill.com |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

Isakson won the Republican primary unopposed.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://sos.georgia.gov/elections/election_results/2010_0720/0010101.htm |title=7/20/2010 - United States Senator, Isakson |publisher=Sos.georgia.gov |date= |accessdate=2010-08-21}}</ref>

On the Democratic side, Labor Commissioner [[Mike Thurmond]] easily defeated RJ Hadley, the chief of staff to the [[Rockdale County, Georgia|Rockdale County]] Commission, and will face Isakson in November.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://sos.georgia.gov/elections/election_results/2010_0720/0010102.htm |title=7/20/2010 - United States Senator, Isakson |publisher=Sos.georgia.gov |date= |accessdate=2010-08-21}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 52.10% of the vote while [[Barack Obama]] received 46.90%, a 5.20-percent margin for the Republican.<ref name="http">{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2008/results/state/#val=GA | work=CNN | title=State Election Results - Election Center 2008 - Elections & Politics from CNN.com | accessdate=May 27, 2010}}</ref>

----

====Mike Crapo of Idaho====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Idaho, 2010}}
Two-term incumbent Republican [[Mike Crapo]] was reelected against only token write-in opposition with 99 percent of the vote in 2004 after Idaho Democrats failed to produce a candidate before the filing deadline.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000155 |title=Profile for Sen. Michael D. Crapo, Idaho Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1951-05-20 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> The Democratic nominee is, Tom Sullivan and the Constitution party nominee is, Randy Lynn Bergquist.

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 61.21% of the vote while [[Barack Obama]] received 35.91%, a 25.30-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref name="http"/>

----

====Chuck Grassley of Iowa====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Iowa, 2010}}
Five-term incumbent Republican [[Chuck Grassley]] was reelected with 70 percent of the vote in 2004. He has announced that he will run for reelection.<ref name=grassley>{{cite web | url = http://www.siouxcityjournal.com/blogs/politically_speaking/?p=821 | title = Grassley won't be surprised to get GOP challenger}}</ref> Conservative Republicans suggest Grassley may face a primary challenge due to his lack of support for their issues.<ref>{{cite web|last=Hancock |first=Jason |url=http://iowaindependent.com/13888/salier-grassley-could-be-primaried |title=Salier: Grassley could be primaried « Iowa Independent |publisher=Iowaindependent.com |date=2008-08-25 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> Potential Republican challengers to Grassley include conservative activist Bill Salier, who said, "Grassley was the dominant force and had an enormous amount of loyalty. That has so eroded out from underneath him" during an interview on WHO-AM radio.<ref>{{cite web|last=Hancock |first=Jason |url=http://iowaindependent.com/18348/salier-renews-threat-of-grassley-primary |title=Salier renews threat of Grassley primary « Iowa Independent |publisher=Iowaindependent.com |date=2008-08-25 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

Former U.S. Attorney [[Roxanne Conlin]] former state representatives [[Bob Krause (Politician)|Bob Krause]] and Tom Fiegen are running for the Democratic nomination.<ref name=conlin>{{cite web | url = http://blogs.desmoinesregister.com/dmr/index.php/2009/11/06/conlin-to-file-paperwork-next-week-to-seek-u-s-senate-seat/ | title = Conlin to file paperwork next week to seek U.S. Senate seat | work = Des Moines Register | date = 2009-11-06 | accessdate = 11-21 | author = Tom Beaumont}}</ref> Krause has said that Grassley's more than half-century in public office is too long. "As a good farmer, Senator Grassley must recognize that 51 years, or 58 years at the end of his term, is a long time to go without rotating crops," Krause told about 50 supporters in Des Moines.<ref name="Krause"/> Krause cited Grassley's support in 1999 for legislation that allowed banks and insurance companies to begin offering other investment products. "Please remember that farmer Grassley was one that opened the barn door and let the cow out at AIG," Krause said.<ref name="Krause">{{cite news|url=http://www.desmoinesregister.com/article/20090329/NEWS09/903290336/1056|title=Democrat says Grassley has been in Senate too long|author=Thomas Beaumont|work=KCCI|date=March 29, 2009}}</ref> On June 8, 2010, Conlin won the Democratic nomination.<ref name="iowadprimary">{{cite web |url=http://www.kcci.com/politics/23834687/detail.html |title=ELECTION RESULTS: June 8 Primary ...view all races - Political News Story - KCCI Des Moines |accessdate=2010-06-09 |author=KCCI}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] received 53.93% of the vote while [[John McCain]] received 44.39%, a 9.54-percent margin of victory for the Democrat.<ref name="http"/>

----

====David Vitter of Louisiana====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Louisiana, 2010}}
One-term incumbent Republican [[David Vitter]] was elected with 51 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000205 |title=Profile for Sen. David Vitter, Louisiana Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1961-05-03 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> This was actually an impressive victory considering Louisiana's election process in 2004 was an [[jungle primary|all-party "open primary"]] where candidates of all parties and no-party ran together in the same election. In receiving 51% of the vote Vitter won the election outright without the need for a runoff election. Former U.S. Representative Chris John, a Democrat, finished second to Vitter with only 29 percent of the vote. Five other candidates completed the field receiving 20 percent combined. Vitter has announced that he will seek reelection,<ref name=vitter2010>{{cite web | url= http://www.iberianet.com/articles/2009/02/20/news/doc499f08ac41b07128910275.txt | title = Vitter discusses economy | date = 2009-02-20 | publisher = The Daily Iberian | author = Heather Miller | accessdate = 2009-02-21}}</ref> though his reelection bid may become complicated by a [[prostitution]] scandal that was revealed in 2007.<ref>[http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2007/07/09/AR2007070902030.html ''[[The Washington Post]]'', ''Senator's Number on 'Madam' Phone List,'' 7/10/07.]</ref>

Secretary of State [[Jay Dardenne]] was a potential challenger to Vitter in the Republican primary,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rollcall.com/issues/54_109/atr/33524-1.html |title=Louisiana: Dardenne Continues to Weigh Vitter Challenge |publisher=Roll Call |date=2009-03-26 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> as was [[pornographic actor|pornographic actress]] [[Stormy Daniels]].<ref>[http://draftstormy.com/]</ref> Retired [[Lieutenant general (United States)|Lieutenant General]] [[Russel L. Honoré]] is strongly considering running in 2010 as a Republican, with one source saying that the ex-[[United States Army|U.S. Army]] commander was "more than 50 percent sure that he will run." <ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.bayoubuzz.com/News/Louisiana/Politics/General_Russell_Honore_To_Run_Vs_David_Vitter_In_Louisiana_US_Race__9439.asp|title=General Russell Honore To Run Vs David Vitter In Louisiana US Race?|author=Christopher Tidmore|accessdate=August 29, 2009}}</ref>

U.S. Representative [[Charles Melancon]] of [[LA-03|Louisiana's 3rd congressional district]], who is the only Democrat in Louisiana's seven-seat delegation in the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. House of Representatives]], announced on August 27, 2009 that he would seek the Democratic nomination for the seat.<ref name=melancon>{{cite web | url = http://www.politico.com/blogs/scorecard/0809/Melancon_running_against_Vitter.html | title = Melancon running against Vitter | publisher = Politico | date = 2009-08-27 | author = Josh Kraushaar}}</ref>

Anthony "Tony G" Gentile of [[Mandeville, Louisiana|Mandeville]] announced on June 16, 2009 that he would seek the Libertarian nomination for the seat. Gentile was a gubernatorial candidate in 2007 and a congressional candidate for [[LA-01|Louisiana's 1st congressional district]] in 2008.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://louisianalibertarian.net/News_%26_Events/Entries/2009/6/16_Anthony_%E2%80%9CTony_G%E2%80%9D_Gentile_Announces_Senate_Bid_as_a_Libertarian.html |title=Anthony “Tony G” Gentile Announces Senate Bid as a Libertarian |publisher=Louisianalibertarian.net |date=2009-06-16 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 58.56% of the vote in Louisiana while [[Barack Obama]] received 39.93%, a 18.63-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref name="http"/>

----

====Richard Burr of North Carolina====
{{Main|United States Senate election in North Carolina, 2010}}
One-term incumbent Republican [[Richard Burr]] was elected with 52 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000293 |title=Profile for Sen. Richard M. Burr, North Carolina Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1955-11-30 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>
Burr is considered a potentially vulnerable incumbent due to his poor approval ratings and low profile statewide. Burr easily won his party's nomination in May 2010 with 80% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.upi.com/Top_News/US/2010/05/04/Burr-wins-in-NC-opponent-undetermined/UPI-63631273027026/ |title=Burr wins in N.C.; opponent undetermined |date=2010-05-04 |accessdate=2010-09-19 |publisher=United Press International}}</ref>

Army veteran and frequent candidate John Hendrix,<ref name="Hendrix">{{cite web|url=http://www.hendrixcampaign.com/ |title=Hendrix Campaign |publisher=Hendrix Campaign |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> attorney Kenneth Lewis, former [[North Carolina Senate|State Senator]] [[Cal Cunningham]] and [[North Carolina Secretary of State|Secretary of State]] [[Elaine Marshall]] are running for the Democratic nomination.<ref name=cunningham>{{cite web | url = http://www.politico.com/blogs/scorecard/1209/Cunningham_announces_race_against_Burr.html?showall | title = Cunningham announces race against Burr | publisher = Politico | date = 2009-12-07 | accessdate = 2009-12-25}}</ref>

On June 22, 2010, Elaine Marshall and Cal Cunningham participated in a runoff primary election for the Democratic nomination as no candidate reached the necessary 40-percent vote needed to win outright. Marshall received approximately 60 percent of the vote to secure the Democratic nomination.

Michael Beitler is running as a Libertarian.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.beitlerforussenate.org/ |title=Dr. Mike Beitler for U.S. Senate |publisher=Beitlerforussenate.org |date= |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[Barack Obama]] narrowly carried North Carolina with 49.70% of the vote while [[John McCain]] received 49.38%, a 0.32-percent margin for the Democrat.<ref name="http"/>

----

====Tom Coburn of Oklahoma====
{{Main|United States Senate election in Oklahoma, 2010}}
One-term incumbent Republican [[Tom Coburn]] was elected with 53 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref name=nyt_south>{{cite news|title=The 2004 Elections: State by State -- South |url=http://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=9A07E0DF173CF937A35752C1A9629C8B63&pagewanted=all |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=2004-11-04 | accessdate=2008-11-06 | first1=Contributing | last1=To | first2=Patrick | last2=Healy | first3=Michael | last3=Janofsky | first4=Michael | last4=Luo | first5=Jennifer | last5=Medina | first6=Robert | last6=Pear | first7=Richard | last7=Prez-Pea | first8=Marc | last8=Santora | first9=Stephanie | last9=Strom | first10=Daniel J. | last10=Wakin}}</ref> Coburn announced on June 1 that he would be running for a second and final term.<ref name="Coburn 2010">{{cite web|url=http://www.tulsaworld.com/news/article.aspx?subjectid=11&articleid=20090601_11_0_TULSAO896445 |title=Video: Coburn to seek second term |work=Tulsa World |date=2009-06-01}}</ref>

Former Republican Party activist Stephen Wallace is challenging Coburn as an [[Independent (politician)|independent]].{{Citation needed|date=October 2009}}

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 65.65 percent of the vote in Oklahoma while [[Barack Obama]] received 34.35%, a 31.30-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref name="http"/>

----

====Jim DeMint of South Carolina====
{{Main|United States Senate election in South Carolina, 2010}}
Incumbent Republican [[Jim DeMint]] was elected with 54 percent of the vote in 2004.<ref>{{cite web|author=Member Profile |url=http://www.cqpolitics.com/wmspage.cfm?docID=profile-000000000439 |title=Profile for Sen. Jim DeMint, South Carolina Republican |publisher=CQ Politics |date=1951-09-02 |accessdate=2010-06-13}}</ref> DeMint has raised $2.2 million for his intended reelection bid.<ref name=demint>{{cite web | url = http://www.greenvilleonline.com/article/20090415/NEWS03/90415024/1069/YOURUPSTATE01 | title = DeMint 2010 war chest: $2.2 million}}</ref> He is considered safe, but Democrats blame him for holding up the confirmation of [[Erroll Southers]] to the [[Transportation Security Administration]]. DeMint also said in July 2009 that the defeat of health care reform would be Obama's [[Battle of Waterloo|Waterloo]].

In a major surprise, unemployed military veteran [[Alvin Greene]] won the Democratic primary to oppose DeMint.  Greene reported no fundraising by mid-October and made few campaign appearances.

[[Tom Clements (politician)|Tom Clements]], candidate of the [[Green Party (United States)|Green Party]] raised more than $45,000, <ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.fec.gov/DisclosureSearch/HSProcessCandList.do?category=stateS_all&stateName=SC&election_yr=2010 |title=2010 House and Senate Campaign Finance for South Carolina |publisher=fec.gov |date= |accessdate=October 18, 2010}}. Next F.E.C. reports are due October 15, 2010.</ref> ran TV and radio advertisements and campaigned around the state. A Winthrop University poll conducted between October 5 and 10 of 741 likely South Carolina voters found Clements running second with 12.2% of the vote against 11.2% for Democrat Alvin Greene and 58.3% for incumbent Jim DeMint. <ref>[http://www.winthrop.edu/news-events/article.aspx?id=13440 Winthrop Poll Shows Haley Ahead and Other Opinions by S.C. Voters] Winthrop Poll, October 13, 2010</ref> <ref>[http://www.winthrop.edu/uploadedFiles/wupoll/ResultsOct2010WinthropPollOnline.pdf Winthrop Poll Shows Haley Ahead and Other Opinions by S.C. Voters] Winthrop Poll Questions and Answers. Page 2. October 13, 2010</ref>.  In a first for a minor party candidate in South Carolina, Clements was endorsed for Senate by the Rock Hill Herald. <ref>[http://www.heraldonline.com/2010/10/30/2572265/clements-for-senate.html Rock Hill Herald: Tom Clements for US Senate: Green Party candidate Tom Clements offers a viable alternative in this race. October 30, 2010.]</ref>


In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 53.87 percent of the vote in South Carolina while [[Barack Obama]] received 44.90 percent, an 8.97-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref name="http"/>

