====John Thune of South Dakota====
{{Main|United States Senate election in South Dakota, 2010}}
One-term incumbent Republican [[John Thune]] was elected with 51 percent of the vote in 2004, ousting former [[Senate Majority Leader]] [[Tom Daschle]].<ref name=nyt_midwest>{{cite news|title=The 2004 Elections: State by State -- Midwest |url=http://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=980CE4DF173CF937A35752C1A9629C8B63&pagewanted=all |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=2004-11-04 | accessdate=2008-11-06 | first1=Contributing | last1=To | first2=Patrick | last2=Healy | first3=Michael | last3=Janofsky | first4=Michael | last4=Luo | first5=Jennifer | last5=Medina | first6=Robert | last6=Pear | first7=Richard | last7=Prez-Pea | first8=Marc | last8=Santora | first9=Stephanie | last9=Strom | first10=Daniel J. | last10=Wakin}}</ref> Thune will likely run for a second term in 2010.<ref name="Thune 2010">{{cite web|url=http://www.ktiv.com/Global/story.asp?S=9478852 | title=Thune says he's likely to run for re-election | publisher = [[KTIV|KTIV.com]] | date=2008-12-06 | accessdate = 2009-01-12}}{{dead link|date=October 2010}}</ref>

In [[United States presidential election, 2008|2008]], [[John McCain]] received 53.16 percent of the vote while [[Barack Obama]] received 44.75 percent, an 8.41-percent margin of victory for the Republican.<ref name="http"/>

